1
00:00:00,292 --> 00:00:01,625
I want to give away Bitcoin.

2
00:00:01,892 --> 00:00:02,842
The prize is ready,

3
00:00:03,008 --> 00:00:04,342
but I don’t know the winner yet.

4
00:00:04,558 --> 00:00:05,708
Or their Bitcoin address.

5
00:00:06,375 --> 00:00:07,875
That’s why I built PrizeKey.

6
00:00:08,025 --> 00:00:09,158
Set up the gift first,

7
00:00:09,258 --> 00:00:10,925
pass its claim on NEAR to the winner,

8
00:00:11,142 --> 00:00:12,825
and let them choose their Bitcoin address.

9
00:00:13,042 --> 00:00:13,750
Let me show you.

10
00:00:14,375 --> 00:00:15,375
In this earlier test,

11
00:00:15,608 --> 00:00:17,942
I funded a gift with 20,000 test sats.

12
00:00:18,308 --> 00:00:19,792
It has its own Bitcoin address.

13
00:00:21,083 --> 00:00:21,958
Once I have a winner,

14
00:00:22,050 --> 00:00:23,067
I enter their NEAR account

15
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:24,267
and pass them the claim.

16
00:00:24,417 --> 00:00:25,958
I don’t need their Bitcoin address.

17
00:00:27,708 --> 00:00:28,958
Now they’re the holder.

18
00:00:29,042 --> 00:00:30,808
They choose where to receive the Bitcoin,

19
00:00:30,808 --> 00:00:32,292
and approve the payout.

20
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:36,167
NEAR Chain Signatures authorizes it,

21
00:00:36,383 --> 00:00:37,875
then the browser sends Bitcoin.

22
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:40,458
This earlier test used a terminal wallet.

23
00:00:41,042 --> 00:00:42,542
Here’s what the recipient sees today.

24
00:00:42,742 --> 00:00:45,458
19,750 test sats delivered,

25
00:00:45,458 --> 00:00:47,625
after a 250-sat fee.

26
00:00:49,417 --> 00:00:50,292
If they want to check,

27
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:52,083
the transaction is right here.

28
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:55,283
Fund the prize first.

29
00:00:55,417 --> 00:00:57,033
NEAR tracks who owns the claim.

30
00:00:57,267 --> 00:00:58,733
Chain Signatures lets that person

31
00:00:58,733 --> 00:01:00,467
approve a native Bitcoin payout.

32
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:02,083
The winner chooses where it goes.
